Ladybird now dispatches every mouse event at a disabled form control,
except a trusted mousedown, mouseup, click, or dblclick, whose
path ends below the first disabled form control on it. Chrome 158,
Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 give the same events, targets, and
paths when run on a disabled button, a span inside one, a disabled
input, and a div in a disabled fieldset (the regression test’s
output is identical in all three), and all three scroll the page on a
wheel over a disabled control.

Blink’s EventPath::AdjustForDisabledFormControl() and WebKit’s
EventPath::adjustForDisabledFormControl() cut the path of those
four trusted events at the first disabled form control. And Gecko’s
nsGenericHTMLFormElement::IsElementDisabledForEvents() lets pointer and
mouse moves, over/out/enter/leave, and wheel events through, and blocks
every other trusted event at a disabled control.

A press in a disabled control still runs its default actions in
Ladybird, as in all three engines when run: A left or a right click on
a disabled input or button moves the focus to the body, a click on a
disabled button keeps the page’s selection, and a click activates an
enabled link or submit button inside a disabled control (a link in a
disabled button, or a submit button in a disabled form-associated custom
element), but not a link around a disabled button, or around a host
whose shadow tree puts a disabled button around its slot.

The engines differ on auxclick and contextmenu at a disabled control:
Chrome dispatches both, WebKit only contextmenu, and Firefox neither.
Ladybird dispatches both, as Chrome does.
